<worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="activity-1-1-5-worksheet">
    <title>1.1.5 Block Matrix Inversion</title>

    <page>

        <p>
            In some cases, matrix partitioning can be used to give us convenient expressions for the inverse of a matrix. Recall that the inverse of <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is a matrix <m>B</m>, that has the same dimensions as <m>A</m> and satisfies
        </p>

        <me>
            AB = BA = I
        </me>

        <p>where <m>I</m> is the <m>n \times n</m> identity matrix. As we will see in the next example, we can use this equation to construct expressions for the inverse of a matrix.</p>
    </page>
</worksheet>